{{also|Wed|we'd|Wed.}}
==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*wedʰ-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|wedden}}, {{m|enm|weddien}}, from {{inh|en|ang|weddian||to pledge; wed}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*waddjōn}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*wadjōną|t=to pledge}}, from {{m|gem-pro|*wadją|t=pledge}}, ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*wedʰ-|t=to pledge}}.

Cognate with {{cog|sco|wed}}, {{m|sco|wod}}, {{m|sco|wad||to wed}}, {{cog|stq|wädje||to bet, wager}}, {{cog|fy|wedzje||to bet, wager}}, Low German and Dutch {{m|nl|wedden||to bet}}, German {{m|de|wetten||to bet}}, Danish {{m|da|vædde||to bet}}, Swedish {{m|sv|vädja||to appeal}}, Icelandic {{m|is|veðja||to bet}}; more distantly, to {{cog|sa|वधू|tr=vadhū́||bride}}. Related also to {{l|en|gage}}, {{l|en|engage}}, and {{l|en|wage}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|wĕdʹ}}, {{IPA|en|/ˈwɛd/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-wed.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛd|s=1}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|+|+|wed|past2=wedded}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To perform the [[marriage]] ceremony for; to join in [[matrimony]].
#: {{ux|en|The priest '''wed''' the couple.}}
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=8|passage=And Adam, '''wedded''' to another Eve, <br> Shall live with her.|year=1873}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To take as one's [[spouse]].
#: {{ux|en|She '''wed''' her first love.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=September 27 2017|author=David Browne|title=Hugh Hefner, 'Playboy' Founder, Dead at 91|titleurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20171202004257/http://www.rollingstone.com/culture/news/hugh-hefner-playboy-founder-dead-at-91-w443922|journal=Rolling Stone
|passage=In 1989, he '''wed''' Playmate Kimberley Conrad, a marriage that ended in 2010. In 2013, he married his younger girlfriend, Crystal Harris, with whom he was still '''wed''' at the time of his death.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To take a spouse.
# {{lb|en|reciprocal}} To take each other as a spouse.
#: {{ux|en|They will '''wed''' in the summer.}}
#* {{RQ:Haggard She|passage=On the rock above was an inscription in three words. Ayesha translated it. It was `'''''Wedded''' in Death''.' What was the life-story of these two, who, of a truth, were beautiful in their lives, and in their death were not divided?}}
# {{lb|en|figuratively|transitive}} To [[join]] or [[commit]] to, more or less permanently, as if in marriage.
#: {{ux|en|I'm not '''wedded''' to this proposal; suggest an alternative.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Romeo and Juliet|act=III|scene=iii|passage=Affliction is enamoured of thy parts, and thou art '''wedded''' to calamity.}}
#* {{RQ:Tillotson Works|sermon=I|passage=Men are '''wedded''' to their lusts.}}
#* {{RQ:Tennyson In Memoriam|XXIII|39|{{...}} When each by turns was guide to each,<br>⁠And Fancy light from Fancy caught,<br>⁠And Thought leapt out to '''wed''' with Thought,<br>Ere Thought could '''wed''' itself with Speech: {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1962|month=April|title=Death from Natural Causes?|journal=Modern Railways|page=218|text=It will be a tragedy if further enterprises of this kind&mdash;for example, the one proposed between South Wales, Bristol and the South Coast via Salisbury&mdash;are now deferred until they, too, are realised too late to make an impact on a public that is too firmly '''wedded''' to the roads to be wooed back to the trains.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2008|author=Bradley Simpson|title=Economists with Guns| page=72| passage={{...}} the PPS paper proposed a political doctrine that '''wedded''' modernization theory to U.S. support for national security states {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|figurative|intransitive}} To take to oneself and support; to [[espouse]].
#* {{RQ:Clarendon History|passage=They positively and concernedly '''wedded''' his cause.}}
# {{lb|en|Northern England|Scotland}} To [[wager]], [[stake]], [[bet]], [[place]] a [[bet]], [[make]] a [[wager]].
#: {{ux|en|I'd '''wed''' my head on that.}}

